	abstract = {Unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) have shown great potential in urban applications. However, achieving multiUAV path planning in complex urban environments remains a signiﬁcant challenge. Existing studies are mostly conducted in two-dimensional or virtual three-dimensional settings and often consider only a single constraint factor, such as risk, wind ﬁeld, or timeliness, which limits the practical applicability of the planning results. To address this issue, this paper proposes a four-dimensional (4D) path planning method for multiple UAVs that integrates risk assessment and layered wind-ﬁeld modeling. First, a three-dimensional (3D) risk map is constructed based on real geographic information system (GIS) data, in which human and property risks are quantiﬁed into a uniﬁed cost function. Second, layered wind ﬁelds and mission time constraints are introduced to extend the decision-making space into four dimensions, while a multi-sampling-based normalization strategy for path length is proposed to achieve a uniﬁed quantiﬁcation of path risks and wind-ﬁeld constraints. Finally, an adaptive hybrid metaheuristic population (AHMP) optimization algorithm is designed to solve the resulting multi-UAV path planning problem in complex urban scenarios. Simulation results show that, in Scenario 1 (riskonly) and Scenario 2 (coupled risk–wind constraints), AHMP achieves consistently better performance across multiple evaluation metrics, in terms of path length, accumulated risk, wind cost, and runtime. In particular, under a high-density 12-UAV setting, AHMP reduces path length by 11.79–20.04\%, accumulated risk cost by 4.88–18.90\%, wind cost by 23.21–32.49\%, and running time by 28.78–55.02\% compared with particle swarm optimization (PSO), improved particle swarm optimization (IPSO), artiﬁcial bee colony (ABC), and PSO with simulated annealing (PSO+SA), while consistently generating collision-free feasible 3D trajectories. Moreover, in Scenario 3 (coupled risk–wind with time constraints), the proposed method further supports synchronized arrival of multiple UAVs, demonstrating its task feasibility under complex coupled constraints.},
